The Observer-Bounded Inference Principle

A Formal Framework for Embedded Inference, Structural Ignorance, and Testable Error Floors

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

---

1. Introduction

A physical theory is always constructed from within. Any observer that builds a model of the universe is itself a subsystem of that universe, subject to the same dynamical laws and resource limitations. This self‑embedding imposes fundamental constraints on what can be known with certainty. Several deep limitations—measurement noise, Gödel incompleteness, computational irreducibility—have been studied separately, but their collective consequence has not been unified into a single conditional impossibility theorem. This chapter provides such a unification.

We model the universe as a deterministic, discrete‑time dynamical system $\mathcal{U}$, and the observer as a finite, embedded agent $O_t$ with a lossy observation channel $\Pi$, an internal model $M_t$, and bounded physical resources. We prove that under physically motivated constraints, the observer cannot achieve verifiable exact reconstruction of the microscopic generative dynamics if the universe is computationally irreducible. The result is conditional: it applies only when the conjunction of constraints holds. We formalise the impossibility as the Observer‑Bounded Inference Principle.

To convert this limit into a measurable quantity, we introduce the time‑dependent robust relative compressibility error $\delta_t^*$, grounded in algorithmic information theory, and outline a falsifiable empirical conjecture. The framework thus provides a rigorous theoretical statement together with an experimentally testable signature of structural ignorance.

---

2. The Universe as a Recursive Deterministic System

2.1 Fundamental Definitions

Definition 2.1 (Universe state and generative function).
The universe at discrete time $t \in \mathbb{N}$ is described by a state $U_t \in \mathcal{U}$, where $\mathcal{U}$ is a (possibly infinite) set. The dynamics is given by a total, deterministic function $F : \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{U}$ such that

U_{t+1} = F(U_t).

The entire trajectory is $U_t = F^{\,t}(U_0)$, where $F^{\,0} = \mathrm{id}$ and $F^{\,t+1} = F \circ F^{\,t}$.

Axiom 2.1 (Determinism). $F$ is total; each state uniquely determines the next.

Axiom 2.2 (Operational closure).
$\mathcal{U}$ is the maximal set of events in the causal past and future light‑cone of the embedded observer $O_t$. There is no operational meaning to “outside” this set. This formalises the structural incompleteness of embedded observation.

Definition 2.2 (Computational irreducibility).
$F$ is computationally irreducible if there exists no computable function $g : \mathbb{N} \to \mathcal{U}$ with $g(t) = F^{\,t}(U_0)$ for all $t$ that has strictly lower worst‑case time complexity than simulating $F$ step‑by‑step. The class of such generating functions is denoted by $\mathcal{I}$.

Axiom 2.3 (Richness / irreducible class).
We restrict our analysis to universes $F \in \mathcal{I}$ that are computationally irreducible and rich enough to support an embedded finite observer. Examples include the elementary cellular automaton Rule 110 (Cook 2004) and Rule 54 (Boccara et al. 2005). Fully integrable or compressible systems (e.g., additive Rule 90) are excluded; they either cannot host complex observers or make the reconstruction problem trivial.

Axiom 2.4 (Microscopic reconstruction target).
The verifiable exact reconstruction that we consider concerns the microscopic generative rule—the local transition function at the finest granularity of the state space (the cell level). Coarse‑grained emergent descriptions, even if computationally reducible, do not constitute a certificate of identity for the underlying microscopic rule. This axiom isolates the impossibility from the observation that some computationally irreducible systems admit reducible effective descriptions at larger scales.

2.2 Computational Irreducibility and Its Consequences

Lemma 2.1 (Irreducibility forbids speed‑up).
If $F \in \mathcal{I}$, then any computable function that yields $F^{\,t}(U_0)$ for all $t$ must have time complexity at least $\Omega(t)$ in the worst case.

Proof. By definition of $\mathcal{I}$, no strictly faster program exists. ∎

In practice, this means the only general way to know the state at time $t$ is to run the system for $t$ steps. No predictive shortcut exists.

