6. Algorithmic Information‑Theoretic Quantification: $\delta_t^*$

To turn this philosophical boundary into a measurable quantity, we introduce the robust relative compressibility error $\delta_t^*$.

6.1 Definition of $\delta_t^*$

Let $K_C(x)$ denote the approximate Kolmogorov complexity of a string $x$ estimated by a lossless compressor $C$. We use a set $\mathcal{C} = \{\mathrm{LZMA},\, \mathrm{bzip2}\}$ of modern compressors that satisfy the normality condition required for reliable normalised compression distance (Cilibrasi & Vitányi 2004). For a single compressor,

K_C(x) = |C(x)| \cdot 8 + \kappa,

where $\kappa$ accounts for the fixed decompressor size (≈ 67 kB). A minimum complexity floor $k_{\min} = 128$ bits is enforced to avoid spurious ratios on very short strings.

Conditional complexity is approximated by concatenation:

K_C(x \mid y) \approx K_C(y \,\Vert\, x) - K_C(y).

Definition 6.1 (Robust relative compressibility error).
Given observation $X_t = \Pi(F(U_t))$ and serialised model weights $Y_t$ (as raw bytes), the error is

\delta_t^* = \sup_{C \in \mathcal{C}} \frac{K_C(X_t \mid Y_t)}{K_C(X_t)},

with the convention $\delta_t^* = 1$ if the denominator is below $k_{\min}$.

This metric measures the fraction of algorithmic content in the observation that remains unexplained by the model. $\delta_t^* \approx 0$ if the model can losslessly compress the observations; $\delta_t^* \approx 1$ if it performs no better than random.

6.2 The Irreducible Error Floor Conjecture

Conjecture 6.1 (Positive irreducible error floor).
For any finite embedded observer in a computationally irreducible universe ($F \in \mathcal{I}$) with lossy observation,

\liminf_{t \to \infty} \delta_t^* \ge \delta_{\min} > 0.

Conversely, for reducible systems ($F \notin \mathcal{I}$, e.g., Rule 90) or when the observation is invertible ($\Pi$ identity), $\delta_t^* \to 0$.

This conjecture gains quantitative backing from a recent result by Azadi et al. (2025), who proved that for a computationally irreducible system the conditional Kolmogorov complexity of the trajectory grows linearly with time: $K(X_t \mid s_0) = \Omega(t)$. Any model that fails to capture the full generative structure will therefore accumulate an irreducible incompressible residue, providing a lower bound on $\delta_{\min}$. The scaling law

\delta_t^*(N) = \delta_\infty + A \cdot N^{-\alpha},

where $N$ denotes model capacity, can be fitted to data; $\delta_\infty$ then serves as an estimate of $\delta_{\min}$. Empirical validation with the reducible Rule 90 demonstrates that $\delta_t^* \to 0$ for a sufficiently large model, confirming that the metric behaves as intended.

