7. Theoretical Grounding and Relation to Prior Work

Our framework builds upon and synthesises several established formalisms:

· Algorithmic Information Dynamics (Abrahão et al. 2022) models observation as mutual perturbation between dynamical systems; our lossy channel $\Pi$ fits naturally into this picture. The robustness of emergent algorithmic information to the choice of compressor justifies the supremum in $\delta_t^*$.
· Observer as System Identification Algorithm (Khan 2025): Axiom 3.1 is a precise statement that the observer is an SIA with bounded Kolmogorov complexity.
· Subsystem Epistemics (Shi 2026) provides the resolution bound that underpins the spatial coarse‑graining $\Pi_{\mathrm{res}}$.
· Good Regulator Theorem (Conant & Ashby 1970; refined by Virgo et al. 2025) clarifies that only a partial homomorphic image of the system is required for regulation—explaining why our impossibility does not forbid successful local adaptation.
· Azadi et al. 2025 independently proved that $K(X_t \mid s_0) = \Omega(t)$ for computationally irreducible systems, directly supporting our irreducibility constraint and the $\delta_t^*$ conjecture.

We explicitly acknowledge the work of Israeli and Goldenfeld (2004), who showed that some computationally irreducible cellular automata, including Rule 110, admit coarse‑grained descriptions that are reducible. Our Axiom 2.4 confines the impossibility to the microscopic level; the existence of emergent reducible descriptions does not provide a certificate of exact identity for the underlying cell‑level rule. The open question of whether an observer could verify a coarse‑grained exact model remains a distinct research direction, not settled here.

---

8. Conclusion

We have formalised the concept of an embedded observer in a deterministic, computationally irreducible universe and proved that, under the conjunction of measurement loss, logical incompleteness, and computational irreducibility, verifiable exact reconstruction of the microscopic generative rule is impossible. The Observer‑Bounded Inference Principle captures this conditional limit. The introduction of the robust algorithmic metric $\delta_t^*$ turns a philosophical boundary into a measurable, empirically testable quantity. The framework thus opens a rigorous research programme on the structural limits of embedded science.

All theorems are conditional; they do not imply absolute unknowability, only that certifiable knowledge of the deepest generative rules is structurally precluded for any observer that is part of the system it studies.


