A. Dynamical Systems and Computational Irreducibility

Definition A.1 (Deterministic discrete‑time dynamical system).
A dynamical system is a pair (\mathcal{U}, F) where \mathcal{U} is a set (the state space) and F: \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{U} is a total function. For an initial state U_0 \in \mathcal{U} the orbit is given by

U_t = F^{\,t}(U_0), \qquad t \in \mathbb{N},
\]  

with F^{\,0} = \mathrm{id} and F^{\,t+1} = F \circ F^{\,t}.

In the concrete setting we consider elementary cellular automata (ECA) on finite lattices with periodic boundary conditions. For a width‑W automaton, \mathcal{U} = \{0,1\}^W and F is induced by a local rule f: \{0,1\}^3 \to \{0,1\} via

(F(s))_i = f(s_{i-1}, s_i, s_{i+1}) \quad (\text{indices modulo } W).

Definition A.2 (Computational irreducibility).
A total function F: \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{U} is computationally irreducible if there exists no computable function g: \mathbb{N} \to \mathcal{U} with g(t) = F^{\,t}(U_0) for all t that has strictly lower worst‑case time complexity than simulating F step‑by‑step. The class of such generators is denoted by \mathcal{I}.

Lemma A.1 (Irreducibility forbids speed‑up).
If F \in \mathcal{I}, then any program that computes F^{\,t}(U_0) for all t requires \Omega(t) operations in the worst case.

Proof. If a program with time complexity o(t) existed, it would provide a strictly faster function than step‑by‑step simulation, contradicting the definition of \mathcal{I}. ∎

Axiom 2.4 (Microscopic reconstruction target).
The verifiable exact reconstruction considered in this work concerns the microscopic generative rule—the local transition function at the finest granularity of the state space. Coarse‑grained emergent descriptions, even if computationally reducible, do not constitute a certificate of identity for the underlying microscopic rule.

