B. Kolmogorov Complexity and Normalized Compression Distance

Fix a universal prefix Turing machine U. For a finite binary string x,

K(x) = \min\{|p| : U(p) = x\}
\]  

is the Kolmogorov complexity of x. For a pair of strings x, y,

K(x \mid y) = \min\{|p| : U(p, y) = x\}
\]  

is the conditional complexity. The invariance theorem (Li & Vitányi 2019) guarantees that K and K(\cdot \mid \cdot) are defined up to an additive constant independent of the chosen universal machine.

Because K is uncomputable, we approximate it by the length of the output of a real‑world lossless compressor. Let \mathcal{C} = \{\mathrm{LZMA},\, \mathrm{bzip2}\} be a set of standard compressors that satisfy the normality condition (Cilibrasi & Vitányi 2004): for any string x, the compressor’s behaviour on x is symmetric and idempotent up to small additive constants, which guarantees that the resulting distance measures are quasi‑universal.

For a compressor C \in \mathcal{C} and string x,

K_C(x) = |C(x)| \cdot 8 + \kappa,
\]  

where \kappa accounts for the fixed decompressor size (approximately 67\,\mathrm{kB} for LZMA, as empirically established). For strings shorter than 4 bytes a minimum complexity floor k_{\min} = 128 bits is enforced to avoid spurious ratios.

Conditional Kolmogorov complexity is approximated by the concatenation method

K_C(x \mid y) \approx K_C(y \,\Vert\, x) - K_C(y),
\]  

where \Vert denotes a distinguishable separator. This method is motivated by the identity K(x \mid y) \approx K(yx) - K(y) that holds up to additive logarithmic corrections for the true Kolmogorov complexity.

Definition B.1 (Normalized Compression Distance).
For a compressor C, the NCD between two strings x, y is

\operatorname{NCD}(x, y) = \frac{C(xy) - \min\{C(x), C(y)\}}{\max\{C(x), C(y)\}}.
\]  

NCD is a quasi‑universal similarity metric that approximates the normalized information distance.

