C. Observation Channel and Information Loss

The observation map \Pi is the composition of two operations:

1. Spatial coarse‑graining \Pi_{\mathrm{res}}: selects a contiguous patch of width w from the global state, i.e., (\Pi_{\mathrm{res}}(s))_i = s_{i+c} for i = 0,\dots,w-1 (indices modulo W).
2. Physical noise \Pi_{\mathrm{phys}}: adds independent Gaussian noise with variance \sigma^2 to each pixel, followed by clipping to [0,1]. In the quantum case this is replaced by a completely positive trace‑preserving map.

Thus \Pi = \Pi_{\mathrm{phys}} \circ \Pi_{\mathrm{res}}. Because \Pi_{\mathrm{res}} and \Pi_{\mathrm{phys}} are non‑injective, the composed channel is strictly lossy.

Proposition C.1 (Information loss).
For any non‑invertible channel \Pi, the mutual information between observation and state satisfies

I(\Pi(U_t); U_t) < H(U_t).

Proof. Let X = \Pi(U). Write the classical stochastic map as a Markov kernel p(x \mid u). The mutual information is

I(X; U) = H(U) - H(U \mid X) = H(U) - \sum_x p(x)\, H(U \mid X = x).
\]  

Since \Pi is not injective, there exist distinct states u_1, u_2 with overlapping output distributions. Consequently there exists at least one observation x_0 with p(x_0) > 0 for which the conditional distribution p(u \mid x_0) has entropy H(U \mid X = x_0) > 0. Hence H(U \mid X) > 0 and the inequality becomes strict. The quantum case follows by writing a Stinespring dilation U \mapsto V(U \otimes |0\rangle\langle0|)V^\dagger and tracing out the environment; the mutual information to the environment is strictly positive when the channel is non‑unitary. ∎

Theorem C.2 (Measurement limit – Theorem 3.2 in main text).
The observation record \mathcal{O}_{\le t} cannot uniquely determine the generative function F. Infinitely many distinct microscopic dynamics F are compatible with any finite lossy observation sequence.

Proof. Because \Pi is non‑injective, pick two distinct initial states U_0^{(1)}, U_0^{(2)} that yield identical observation sequences for all t (e.g., they differ only in cells permanently outside the observed patch). The evolution can differ arbitrarily in those unobserved cells, leading to different transition functions F_1, F_2 that agree on all observed data. Hence F is not uniquely identifiable. ∎

