D. Formal Verifiability Constraint

Theorem D.1 (Gödel–Turing barrier – Theorem 4.1).
Assume the observer’s inference procedure is formalisable in a recursively axiomatisable theory T that contains elementary arithmetic. If F is Turing‑complete (e.g., Rule 110), then there exists no proof in T that M = F for any candidate model M.

Proof. Because F is Turing‑complete, there exists a computable reduction \phi that maps any Turing machine \mathcal{M} and input x to an initial configuration U_0 of the cellular automaton such that the evolution of F from U_0 eventually produces a special marker symbol iff \mathcal{M}(x) halts.

Now suppose, for contradiction, that T \vdash (M = F). Since M is a total computable function, one can use M to simulate the evolution from \phi(\mathcal{M}, x) and check for the marker. This constitutes a decision procedure for the halting problem within T, contradicting Turing’s undecidability theorem. Therefore no such proof can exist. ∎

Corollary D.2. Even if the observer’s model happens to be identically equal to F, it can never obtain a finite internal certificate of that identity.

