E. Irreducibility and Exhaustive Testing

Theorem E.1 (Irreducibility prevents exhaustive verification – Theorem 4.2).
If F \in \mathcal{I} and the observer is an embedded, finite agent, then the observer cannot test a candidate model M against all possible input states within any finite time.

Proof. To verify that M(U) = F(U) for all U \in \mathcal{U}, the observer would need to evaluate F on every state. Because F is computationally irreducible, Lemma A.1 implies that computing F(U) for an arbitrary state U cannot be done faster than by simulating the dynamics step‑by‑step from a known related state. The number of states |\mathcal{U}| = 2^W is super‑exponential in the observer’s own scale, and each evaluation requires at least one time step of the universe. An observer with a finite proper time (Axiom 3.1) cannot complete 2^W distinct evaluations. Even if the observer restricted attention to states reachable along its own trajectory, it would still require more steps than the universe has yet evolved to cover all possibilities, and irreducibility precludes any shortcut to compute future states before they occur. Hence exhaustive empirical testing is impossible. ∎

