F. Full Proof of the Observer‑Bounded Inference Principle

We restate the main result with all hypotheses made explicit.

Definition F.1 (Verifiable exact reconstruction).
An observer O_t achieves verifiable exact reconstruction of F if it outputs a pair (M, V) where:

· M : \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{U} is a computable function,
· V is a finite proof in a recursively axiomatisable theory T (extending elementary arithmetic) of the statement \forall U \in \mathcal{U}\; M(U) = F(U).

Theorem F.2 (Observer‑Bounded Inference – Theorem 5.1).
Assume:

1. \mathcal{U} is an irreducible universe: F \in \mathcal{I} (Axiom 2.3).
2. The observer O_t is embedded (O_t \subset U_t) and its observation channel \Pi is non‑invertible (Definition 3.1, Proposition C.1).
3. The observer has finite computational capacity and finite proper time (Axiom 3.1).
4. Reconstruction targets the microscopic rule (Axiom 2.4).

Then O_t cannot achieve verifiable exact reconstruction of F. Formally,

O_t \subset \mathcal{U} \;\Longrightarrow\; \neg\exists\, (M, V) \text{ within } O_t \text{ such that } V \vdash (M = F).

Proof. For (M, V) to constitute a verifiable exact reconstruction, the following three necessary conditions must all be satisfied:

(i) Full observability. The observer must have gathered sufficient information to discriminate the true microscopic F from all alternatives.
(ii) Formal verifiability. The formal system T must be able to prove that M equals F.
(iii) Exhaustive empirical coverage. The observer must be able to test M against all possible inputs U \in \mathcal{U}; otherwise, even with a correct candidate, the observer cannot be certain that it matches F on unseen states.

We now show that each condition fails under the stated assumptions.

Condition (i) fails by Theorem C.2. The channel \Pi is lossy, so the observation record does not uniquely identify F. Hence the observer cannot rule out infinitely many alternative functions.

Condition (ii) fails by Theorem D.1. Because F is Turing‑complete, no recursively axiomatisable theory containing arithmetic can prove the equivalence of M and F. The observer cannot obtain an internal finite proof.

Condition (iii) fails by Theorem E.1. Computational irreducibility, combined with the observer’s finite resources and proper time, precludes exhaustive testing of the candidate model against all states.

Since at least one of the necessary conditions is violated, the conjunction cannot be satisfied. Therefore no verifiable exact reconstruction exists. ∎

Corollary F.3 (Observer‑Bounded Inference Principle).
Embedded finite observers with lossy observation channels cannot verifiably reconstruct the exact microscopic generative dynamics of computationally irreducible systems.

