G. Robust Relative Compressibility Error \delta_t^*

Definition G.1. Let X_t = \Pi(F(U_t)) be the observation (converted to bytes) and Y_t the serialised model weights. For a compressor C,

\delta_t^C = \frac{K_C(X_t \mid Y_t)}{K_C(X_t)},

with the convention \delta_t^C = 0 if the denominator is below k_{\min}. The robust error is the supremum over the compressor set:

\delta_t^* = \sup_{C \in \mathcal{C}} \delta_t^C.

Basic properties.

· 0 \le \delta_t^* \le 1.
· If the model can losslessly compress the observations, \delta_t^* \to 0.
· If the model provides no compression benefit, \delta_t^* \approx 1.

Empirical grounding. For the reducible Rule 90 with a sufficiently large observer and noise‑free full observation, \delta_t^* vanishes, confirming the metric’s intended behaviour. For irreducible systems, a positive floor \delta_{\min} > 0 is expected to persist even as model capacity grows.

Conjecture G.2 (Positive irreducible error floor).
If F \in \mathcal{I} and \Pi is lossy, then

\liminf_{t \to \infty} \delta_t^* \ge \delta_{\min} > 0.

This conjecture is supported by the independent result of Azadi et al. (2025) that for a computationally irreducible system the conditional Kolmogorov complexity of the trajectory grows linearly: K(X_t \mid s_0) = \Omega(t). Any model that fails to capture the full generative structure will therefore accumulate an incompressible residue, providing a quantitative lower bound on \delta_{\min}. The scaling law

\delta_t^*(N) = \delta_\infty + A \cdot N^{-\alpha},

where N is model capacity, can be fitted to estimate the asymptotic floor \delta_\infty.

