H. Halting Classifier and Scaling Law

For completeness we record the three‑valued halting classifier H_O used in the empirical protocol. Given a window of robust errors \{\delta_t\} of length w = 100 and a trend length \tau = 50, the classifier outputs:

H_O(t)=
\begin{cases}
\mathrm{Stable} & \text{if } \operatorname{std}( \delta_{t-\tau+1},\dots,\delta_t ) < \varepsilon,\\[4pt]
\mathrm{Diverging} & \text{if } \bar{\delta}_{\text{recent}} - \bar{\delta}_{\text{older}} > \varepsilon,\\[4pt]
\mathrm{Undecided} & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}

with \varepsilon = 0.02. The stability condition is motivated by martingale convergence: if \delta_t converges to a limit, its increments shrink and the variance becomes small. The scaling law fitting is performed by nonlinear least‑squares using the model \delta_t^*(N) = \delta_\infty + A \cdot N^{-\alpha}, with bounds to ensure physical interpretability. This procedure yields an estimate of the irreducible floor \delta_\infty for different universes, directly testing Conjecture G.2.